; regression test for a severe, general robustness bug (independent of the specific
; bracket-lexing bug in bracket-adjacency-lexes-correctly.kurt): if a file ends while a
; statement is still incomplete (e.g. a genuinely unclosed bracket -- a plain typo, nothing
; to do with the lexer), `read_eval_loop`'s file-reading branch just does `if not new_line:
; break` on EOF -- silently discarding the incomplete statement and returning success,
; with NO indication that the rest of the file (including whatever its real last statement
; was supposed to be) was never actually read. `Proof checked` printed with exit code 0 for
; a file that was silently truncated is exactly the kind of thing that must never happen:
; if kurt says a proof is fine, it must be fine. Fixed by raising a clear `ParseError`
; instead of breaking when EOF is hit while still mid-parse (`continued == True`).
; (the file that ends inside the statement is a helper, loaded here)
; The message names the line where the statement started (until 2026-10-14: the line after the
; end of the file, "around line 5").
expect "ParseError" "unexpected end of file while still parsing the statement that started at line 3"
    load "helpers/eof-mid-statement.kurt"
